================================================================================
Have I Been Pwned — Pwned Passwords
================================================================================

The password breach-corpus screen
(services/api/internal/breachlist/breached-passwords.bin, NIST SP 800-63B
§5.1.1.2) is designed around Have I Been Pwned (Pwned Passwords), created and
maintained by Troy Hunt. The attribution below is split by what is ACTUALLY
embedded today versus the intended production corpus, because the two have
different licensing footprints.

--- Currently embedded (bootstrap seed) ---

The shipped asset is a curated bootstrap seed of the most-prevalent weak/
breached passwords. These are public-knowledge strings (e.g. "password",
"123456", "qwerty") that appear across many public sources (RockYou, SecLists,
NCSC lists, etc.); they were assembled by AxiaOps and are NOT mechanically
extracted from the HIBP corpus. As public-domain plaintext, the seed itself
carries no third-party licence or attribution obligation. See
docs/breachlist-provenance.md for the manifest (N, SHA-256, lineage).

--- Intended production corpus (future swap) ---

The production corpus is the full HIBP top-N most-prevalent SHA-1 digest list,
swapped in via the one-step regeneration in docs/breachlist-provenance.md. THAT
data is licensed CC BY 4.0 and the attribution below becomes mandatory the
moment it is embedded:

    Source:  Have I Been Pwned — Pwned Passwords
             https://haveibeenpwned.com/Passwords
    Author:  Troy Hunt
    Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
             https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

In both cases only the data (compromised-password SHA-1 digests) is embedded;
the SHA-1 digest functions purely as a lookup index, not as a password-storage
primitive (AxiaOps stores passwords with argon2id). The
HaveIBeenPwned/PwnedPasswordsDownloader tool is used only to *generate* the
asset and is NOT embedded, so its separate licence does not propagate into the
AxiaOps binaries.
